    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Is This a Good Offer?\n\n**Yes, it is a solid offer, but it is not top-of-band.** \n\n* **The Equity Grant ($1.08M over 4 years):** This is strong for an initial L6 Product Manager offer. \n* **Vesting Mechanics (GSU Front-loading):** Google typically uses a front-loaded vesting schedule (either **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%** or **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** rather than a flat 25% each year):\n  * **Year 1:** Base ($235k) + Bonus ($47k) + Equity (~33\u201338% = $356k\u2013$410k) = **~$638k \u2013 $692k**.\n  * **Year 2:** **~$628k \u2013 $638k**.\n  * **Years 3 & 4:** Equity drops significantly (Year 4 equity alone falls to ~$108k\u2013$130k). While annual performance refreshes stack over time to backfill this, your guaranteed baseline drops considerably.\n* **The Gaps:**\n  * **Base Salary ($235k):** It is actually a slight pay cut from your current Meta base ($240k), and sits near the middle of Google's posted band ($183k\u2013$271k).\n  * **Sign-on Bonus ($0):** Leaving Meta means forfeiting upcoming bonus cycles and unvested equity; entering without a sign-on bonus leaves significant money on the table.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Should You Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n**Do not accept as-is, and do not walk away yet\u2014you should negotiate.**\n\nYou are in the strongest possible negotiating position:\n1. You were **passively recruited**.\n2. You are **happy and well-compensated at Meta** ($526k/yr run-rate).\n3. Google has already invested substantial hiring committee resources into clearing you for an L6 PM bar.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to Ask For and Specific Target Numbers\n\nWhen negotiating with Google, the easiest levers to move are **Sign-on Bonus** and **Equity**, followed by **Base Salary**.\n\n| Component | Current Meta | Google Initial Offer | **Your Counter-Offer Ask** | Realistic Expected Landing |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** | $245,000 \u2013 $250,000 |\n| **Bonus (Target)** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | **$51,000 \u2013 $52,000** *(tied to base)* | ~$49,000 \u2013 $50,000 |\n| **4-Yr RSUs** | ~$250,000/yr | $1,080,000 total | **$1,250,000 \u2013 $1,300,000** | $1,150,000 \u2013 $1,200,000 |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | N/A | $0 | **$80,000 \u2013 $100,000** | $50,000 \u2013 $75,000 |\n\n#### Why These Specific Targets:\n1. **Base Salary ($255k\u2013$260k):** The range goes up to $271k. Moving you above your current $240k base is standard practice when poaching a senior IC.\n2. **Sign-on Bonus ($80k\u2013$100k):** Google frequently uses one-time sign-on bonuses to bridge unvested equity gaps and incentive timing.\n3. **Equity ($1.25M+):** Top-of-band L6 PM equity packages can reach $1.2M\u2013$1.3M+ in competitive scenarios.\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Frame the Conversation (Talking Points)\n\nYou can schedule a brief call with your recruiter and use framing similar to this:\n\n> *\"Thank you for putting this offer together. I really enjoyed the team and the scope of the L6 role, but as I shared early on, I wasn\u2019t actively looking and am in a great spot at Meta making ~$530k/yr with strong momentum.*\n>\n> *Looking closely at the numbers, the base salary of $235k is below my current $240k base, and there isn't a sign-on component to help offset the unvested equity and bonus I\u2019d be walking away from. To make this move compelling enough to leave a team I\u2019m very happy with, I'm looking for:*\n> - *A **base salary of $255,000***\n> - *An **equity grant of $1.25M over 4 years***\n> - *A **sign-on bonus of $80,000\u2013$100,000***\n>\n> *If we can get to these numbers, I\u2019d be excited to sign and make the transition.\"*\n\n### Next Steps\nLet them take the counter-proposal back to the compensation committee. Google rarely pulls an offer for a polite, market-calibrated counter from a strong passive candidate. If they meet you halfway (e.g., $245k\u2013$250k base, $1.15M+ equity, $50k+ sign-on), you will have secured a competitive Year 1 package ($700k+) that significantly beats your current compensation.",
